2 CONT c)         [--- Komnenos .  His parentage is indicated by Georgios Phrantzes who records an incident during the siege of Patras, dated to 29 Mar 1429, when he rescued Konstantinos Palaiologos [the future Emperor Konstantinos XI] b
2 CONC ut was captured after falling from his horse, a thoroughbred which "ameras" [the emir, unspecified] had given to “Isaacio Asani” who had later given it to “Georgio Philanthropeno genero [γαμβρω] suo” who, in turn had given it to “
2 CONC Comneno consobrino [ανεψιω], Cantacuzeni protostratoris genero [γαμβρω]”[130].  One explanation for this person “Comneno” being “ανεψιω” of Georgios Philanthropenos is that the relationship was through Anna Philanthropena, second 
2 CONC wife of Emperor Manuel III.  The question has been explored fully in a recent article by Thierry Ganchou[131].  The circumstances of the flight of this person to Galata are unknown, but given the rivalries within the Trebizond imp
2 CONC erial family it would not be surprising if he had considered that removing himself from the court was the option which better guaranteed his safety.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (before 29 Mar 1429) EUDOKIA Kantakouzena, daughter of MANUEL Kantakouzenos & his wife ---.  The Masarelli Vatican manuscript names Andreas and Eudokia as the children of Manuel & his wife, stating that Eudokia married a g
2 CONC randson of the emperor of Trebizond but died without issue[132].  Her marriage appears to be confirmed by Georgios Phrantzes who records an incident during the siege of Patras, dated to 29 Mar 1429, when he rescued Konstantinos Pa
2 CONC laiologos [the future Emperor Konstantinos XI] but was captured after falling from his horse, a thoroughbred which "ameras" [the emir, unspecified] had given to “Isaacio Asani” who had later given it to “Georgio Philanthropeno gen
2 CONC ero [γαμβρω] suo” who, in turn had given it to “Comneno consobrino [ανεψιω], Cantacuzeni protostratoris genero [γαμβρω]”[133].] 
2 CONT
