1 TITL Sheikh
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} leader of the Safaviyya Sufi order and first of the Shirvanshahs
2 CONT
2 CONT Shaykh Haydar or Sheikh Haydar was the successor of his father (Shaykh Junayd) as leader of the Safaviyya from 1460-1488. Haydar maintained the policies and political ambitions initiated by his father. Under Sheikh Haydar, the Saf
2 CONC aviyya became crystallized as a political movement with an increasingly extremist heterodox Twelver Shi'i coloring and Haydar was viewed as a divine figure by his followers.[1] Shaykh Haydar was responsible for instructing his fol
2 CONC lowers to adopt the scarlet headgear of 12 gores commemorating The Twelve Imams, which led to them being designated by the Turkish term Qizilbash "Red Head".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1469-70, Haydar was installed in Ardabil by his uncle Uzun Hassan(his mother's brother), who had defeated Jahan Shah of Kara Koyunlu dynasty at the Battle of Chapakchur and established his own authority over its former domains.
2 CONC  The Safaviyya return to Ardabil prompted an influx of Haydar’s followers from northern Syria and eastern Anatolia to Ardabil to be beside him.[3] Soon after, in 1471-1472, Haydar married Uzun Hassan’s daughter Halima, daughter of
2 CONC  Despina Khatun, daughter of John IV of Trebizond).
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage produced three sons: Sultan Ali Safawi, Ibrahim Mirza and Ismail I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably knowing that his forces were not strong enough to try conclusions with the Aq Qoyunlu, Haydar let his forces on a razzia against the "infidels" of Circassia and Dagestan. This involved crossing the territory of the Shirva
2 CONC nshahs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following year (1488), he was defeated and killed in Derbent by the combined forces of the Shirvanshah ruler Farrukh Yassar and the Ak Koyunlu Sultan Ya'qub ibn Uzun Hassan, during one of his military expeditions while tryi
2 CONC ng to avenge his father's death, and was succeeded by his son Sultan Ali Safawi as the leader of the Safaviyya.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his death, his tomb in Ardabil became a place of pilgrimage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====
2 CONT
2 CONT [Being envious of Sultan Haydar's increasing power, Ya'qiib Mirza seized the 
2 CONT opportunity of helping Farrukh Yasgr, the ShirwSn Shah, against Sultan Haydar, who  had set out from Ardabil for the invasion of CharkaS,' and Daghistan. In the ensuing  battle, on Rajah 20, 893/July 1, 1488, Sultan Haydar lost hi
2 CONC s life, and his son and  successor, Sultan 'All Mirza aroused the suspicion of Ya'qiib Mirza, whereupon, in  the end of Rabi' II, 894/end of March 1489, Sultan 'All Mirza, his brothers, Ibrahim  Mirza and Isma'il Mirza, and their 
2 CONC mother, were imprisoned in the fort of Istakhr in  Fars.] ref: https://archive.org/stream/HistoryOfShahIsmailSafawi-GhulamSarwar/119900259-History-of-Shah-Isma-il-Safawi-Ghulam-Sarwar_djvu.txt
