1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luarsab_I_of_Kartli '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Luarsab I''' (Georgian: ლუარსაბ I) (born between 1502-1509 – died 1556 or 1558), of the Bagrationi dynasty, was a king of the Georgian Kingdom of Kartli from 1527 to 1556 or from 1534 to 1558. Persistent in his resistance again
2 CONC st Safavid Persian aggression, he was killed in the Battle of Garisi.[1] 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT (Migration and the Transplantation of the Tribal Elements by Maeda Hirotake)
2 CONT From chronicle sources, it is clear that immigrants into the Caucasus
2 CONT largely consisted of qezelbāsh tribes. Turkish tribal forces in Anatolia
2 CONT known as qezelbāsh made a great contribution to the establishment of the
2 CONT Safavid dynasty. The earlier Safavid Shāhs settled qezelbāsh tribes along
2 CONT two rivers, the Aras and Kor. They rewarded their loyalists with favorable
2 CONT lands for their services.12
2 CONT Of course it was not only for their livestock. Their move was
2 CONT politically backed up by Safavid authority. There were several indigenous
2 CONT dynasties who strongly opposed the Safavid rule. For example Luarsab I,
2 CONT king of Kartli of Georgia, resisted over some thirty years but finally was
2 CONT killed in 963–964/1556–1557 by Shāhverdī Khān Ziyādoghlū Qājār who
2 CONT was supposedly the first governor-in-general (beglarbegī) of Qarābāgh.
2 CONT http://src-h.slav.hokudai.ac.jp/coe21/publish/no10_ses/09_maeda.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Life=
2 CONT
2 CONT The eldest son of David X, he succeeded on the abdication of his uncle, George IX, in 1527 (more accepted date) or 1534.
2 CONT
2 CONT When young, he distinguished himself as a commander in his father’s army, particularly at the Battle of Teleti (1522), won by a Persian invasion army in spite of heavy losses. He established close contacts with Bagrat III of Imere
2 CONC ti, king of Imereti (western Georgia) and married in 1526 his daughter. A year later, he was crowned king of Kartli and launched a series of measures to strengthen the kingdom’s defence capacity amid the ongoing war between Safavi
2 CONC d Persia and Ottoman Turkey (1514-1555). In alliance with Bagrat of Imereti, Luarsab fought both empires trying to preserve his independence and reestablish close cooperation between various Georgian polities. In 1435, Bagrat conq
2 CONC uered a pro-Ottoman southern Georgian principality of Samtskhe, granting its province Javakheti to Kartli. The Kartlian-Imeretian alliance was soon joined by another Georgian monarch, Levan I of Kakheti. However, the 1541 invasion
2 CONC  by the Persian shah Tahmasp I forced Levan out of a Georgian coalition, it left most of Kartli in ruins, and the capital Tbilisi garrisoned by a Persian force. The year 1545 brought another misfortune: a combined army of the Imer
2 CONC etian and Kartlian kings were crushed by the Ottomans at the Battle of Sokhoista and expelled from Samtskhe. From 1547 to 1554, Kartli suffered three more invasions by Tahmasp of Persia, who overran the country, but failed to forc
2 CONC e the king into submission. The Treaty of Amasya, 1555, between the Safavids and Ottomans left Georgia divided between these empires, with the east under Persian domination and the west under Turkish control. Luarsab, however, did
2 CONC  not recognise the treaty, and continued desperate guerilla war against the Persian occupation forces virtually holding them under siege. In order to subdue the stubborn Georgian monarch, Shah Tahmasp sent Shahverdi Sultan, beglar
2 CONC beg of Ganja and Karabakh, with a large army. Luarsab and his son, Simon, met the invaders at the Battle of Garisi. A heavy fight resulted in a Georgian victory, but Luarsab was mortally wounded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Luarsab was buried at the Svetitskhoveli Cathedral at Mtskheta.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family and children=
2 CONT
2 CONT Luarsab I married on March 25, 1526 Tamar of Imereti, daughter of king Bagrat III of Imereti. They had eight children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # A son, who died in 1536 and was buried at Mtskheta
2 CONT # Simon I, King of Kartli
2 CONT # David XI, King of Kartli
2 CONT # Vakhtang (c. 1546–1605), sometime governor of Akhaldaba and Dirbi. He was married to the certain Tinatin and had had a son, Teimuraz-Mirza, and a daughter, Tamar. Teimuraz-Mirza's son, Luarsab, (died in 1650), was adopted by Kin
2 CONC g Rostom of Kartli as his heir in 1639. Tamar was married to Prince Paremuz Amilakhvari.
2 CONT # Alexander (fl. 1546–1573)
2 CONT # Levan
2 CONT # A daughter, married Kekaoz Chkheidze (fl. 1570–1590). Their son Gorgasal Chkheidze (fl. 1590–1629) was married to a daughter of Giorgi Saakadze.
2 CONT # A daughter, married Giorgi, son of Levan of Kakheti.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Maeda, Hirotake (2006). "The forced migrations and reorganisation of the regional order in the Caucasus by Safavid Iran: Preconditions and developments described by Fazli Khuzani". In Ieda, Osamu; Uyama, Tomohiko. Reconstruction
2 CONC  and interaction of Slavic Eurasia and its neighbouring worlds (PDF). Slavic Eurasian Studies, No.10. Sapporo: Slavic Research Centre, Hokkaido University. p. 241. ISBN 4938637391.
2 CONT
