1 NOTE {geni:about_me} from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dedisimedi '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Dedisimedi''' (Georgian: დედისიმედი; died c. 1595) was a Georgian noblewoman of the House of Mukhrani, a collateral branch of the royal Bagrationi dynasty. She was princess consort of Samtskhe as wife of Kaikhosro II Jaqeli (r.
2 CONC  1545–1573) and regent for her son Kvarkvare VI Jaqeli (r. 1573–1581). She played a leading role in a civil war that plagued Samtskhe from 1576 to 1578. After the Ottoman takeover of her principality, Dedisimedi retired to Kartli,
2 CONC  leaving the government to her son, Manuchar II Jaqeli, who continued to rule as an Ottoman pasha. "Dedisimedi" means "hope of mother" in Georgian.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Family background and name
2 CONT * 2 Marriage
2 CONT * 3 Regency
2 CONT * 4 Children
2 CONT * 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT =Family background and name=
2 CONT
2 CONT Dedisimedi was born into the princely family of Mukhrani, the Mukhranbatoni, a collateral branch of the royal house of Kartli. The sources differ as to her parents. The 16th-century Chronicle of Meskhian Psalter as well as the 18t
2 CONC h century Georgian historian Prince Vakhushti and a church inscription from Vale suggest that Dedisimedi's father was Bagrat, son of King Constantine II. In contrast, Vakhushti's contemporaneous editor of the Georgian Chronicles, 
2 CONC Beri Egnatashvili, makes her, erroneously, daughter of Bagrat's son Ashotan and, hence, sister of Saint Ketevan the Martyr.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT As Egnatashvili claims, on her marriage into the Jaqeli family she was given the name Dedisimedi—literally, "a mother's hope"—already known in the family earlier: so was named a consort of Kvarkvare IV Jaqeli, who died in 1489. Wh
2 CONC en relating events in her widowhood, one of the editions of the Georgian Chronicles refers to the dowager princess as "Deborah, formerly Dedisimedi". This gave rise to a hypothesis that, at some point of her life and probably afte
2 CONC r her husband's death in 1573, Dedisimedi might have become a nun under the name of Deborah.[1] Many modern scholars such as K. Sharashenidze and Sh. Lomsadze have dismissed such a possibility on account of her energetic involveme
2 CONC nt in war and politics.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage=
2 CONT
2 CONT Dedisimedi married Prince Kaikhosro II Jaqeli, a 22-year-old prince-atabag of Samtskhe, in 1545. Kaikhosro's rule over Samtskhe, one of the breakaway states of the Kingdom of Georgia, was marred by incessant Iranian–Ottoman rivalr
2 CONC y over the territory, uneasy relations with neighboring Georgian polities, and internecine feuds. Installed through the Ottoman intervention, Kaikhosro ended up his reign, watching the western part of his principality being assimi
2 CONC lated by the Ottomans and the eastern moiety—where he preferred to stay—being subjected to Iran. He died while visiting the court of Shah Tahmasp I at Qazvin in 1573.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Not much is known about Dedisimedi's life during the turbulent years of Kaikhosro's rule. In a period of ascendancy of the Muslim empires, she patronized Christianity and rebuilt the Church of Theotokos of Vale in the years of 156
2 CONC 1–1563. She is also credited to have been one of the authors of the Chronicle of the Meskhetian Psalter, a fragmented account of the 1561–1587 events in Samtskhe (Meskheti) attached to a Psalter manuscript.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Regency=
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of Kaikhosro, Kvarkvare VI, the eldest of his eight children with Dedisimedi, succeeded to the principate of Samtskhe. Since he was still young and inexperienced, the actual government of the country was taken over by
2 CONC  Dedisimedi and the nobleman Varaza Shalikashvili, whose sister was a favorite wife in Shah Tahmasp's harem.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1574, the Kakhetian nobleman Cholokashvili—in a quest to divert the shah's attention from the affairs of Kakheti—successfully spread rumors to convince Dedisimedi of Shalikashvili's perfidy. The princess dowager—described by th
2 CONC e historian Vakhushti as imperious, jealous, and cruel—had Shalikashvili murdered on charges of plotting with the Iranians. Shah Tahmasp reacted to the killing of his brother-in-law and ally by invading Samtskhe. Dedisimedi and he
2 CONC r sons were put into flight to the mountains of Adjara and many of their castles were conferred on the murdered Shalikashvili's Islamized son, Kokola (Mahmud Khan). As the shah left, Dedisimedi was able to resume her reign, but an
2 CONC  uprising and an attempt on Kvarkvare VI followed. From 1576 through 1578, civil war raged through eastern Samtskhe between the Jaqeli faction and the aristocratic opposition led by Kokola Shalikashvili, leaving towns such as Tmog
2 CONC vi in ruins.[3][4] Dedisimedi was personally involved in fighting, directing operations at Queli and Tmogvi.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, the Jaqeli prevailed, but the decimated country became an easy target for the powerful thrust of the Ottoman army under Lala Kara Mustafa Pasha's command in August 1578. The nobles of Samtskhe submitted after a token r
2 CONC esistance and Lala Pasha made a common cause with Dedisimedi's more able and calculating son Manuchar, a co-regent with his mother.[3] The Jaqeli accepted Ottoman suzerainty. Lala Pasha met Dedisimedi at her base at Okros-Tsikhe (
2 CONC Altunkal'a) and politely made it clear that her elder sons should become Muslims. In 1579, both Kvarkvare and Manuchar repaired to Constantinople and the latter pompously converted to Islam under the name of Mustafa. Following Kva
2 CONC rkvare's death in 1581, Manuchar-Mustafa became an Ottoman governor of a reduced Samtskhe, that is, pasha of Childir.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Dedisimedi left the Ottoman-occupied Samtskhe in 1585 and retired to Akhaldaba, in Kartli, where Manuchar, having revolted from the Ottoman authority, had also taken refuge. Early in 1586, Manuchar acceded to the Ottoman demands a
2 CONC nd sent Dedisimedi and her grandson, Kaikhosro's son, to Samtskhe. Thereafter, she disappears from the contemporary historical records, save for the Ottoman fiscal documents of 1595, listing the estates formerly belonging to her i
2 CONC n Samtskhe.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT Dedisimedi had eight children with Kaikhosro II:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Kvarkvare VI
2 CONT * Manuchar II
2 CONT * Ivane the Fair-Eyed (tvalmshvenieri)
2 CONT * Beka
2 CONT * Basil
2 CONT * Mzechabuk
2 CONT * Elene
2 CONT * Tamar
2 CONT
