1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Badi-al Zaman Mirza Safavi was a Safavid prince, who was the son of prominent military leader Bahram Mirza Safavi, who was the youngest son of Ismail I, the founder of the Safavid empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Badi-al Zaman had two brothers named Ibrahim Mirza and Sultan Husayn Mirza.[1] When Badi-al Zaman's father died in 1549, he, along with his other siblings were taken care of by Tahmasp, who even announced Badi-al Zaman as his own 
2 CONC son. Badi al-Zaman was thereafter appointed as the governor of Sistan, and married Pari Khan Khanum (who was at that time 10 years old). However, since she was Tahmasp's favored daughter, she was not allowed to go alongside her hu
2 CONC sband to Sistan.[2] On 26 March 1577, Badi-al Zaman Mirza was assassinated in Qandahar on the orders of shah Ismail II (r. 1576–77).
