1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeb-un-Nissa
2 CONT
2 CONT Zeb-un-Nissa Shahzadi of the Mughal Empire
2 CONT
2 CONT Zeb-un-Nissa (Persian: زیب النساء مخفی‎)[1] (15 February 1638 – 26 May 1702)[2] was a Mughal princess, the eldest child of Emperor Aurangzeb and his chief consort Dilras Banu Begum. She was also a poet, who wrote under the pseudon
2 CONC ym of "Makhfi" (مخفی, "Hidden One"). Imprisoned by her father in the last 20 years of her life at Salimgarh Fort, Delhi, Princess Zeb-un-Nissa is remembered as a poet, and her writings were collected posthumously as Diwan-i-Makhfi
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Zeb-un-Nissa ("Ornament of Womankind"),[4] the eldest child of Prince Muhi-ud-Din (the future emperor Aurangzeb), was born on 15 February 1638 in Daulatabad, Deccan, exactly nine months after the marriage of her parents. Her mothe
2 CONC r, Dilras Banu Begum, was Aurangzeb's first wife and chief consort, and was a princess of the prominent Safavid dynasty; the ruling dynasty of Iran (Persia).[5][6] Zeb-un-Nissa was her father's favourite daughter,[7] and because o
2 CONC f this she could compel him to pardon people who had offended him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Education and accomplishments
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father charged Hafiza Mariam, one of the women of the court, with the education of Zeb-un-Nissa. She seems to have inherited her father's keenness of intellect and literary tastes because Zeb-un-Nissa memorized the Quran in th
2 CONC ree years and became a Hafiza at age seven. This occasion was celebrated by her father with a great feast and public holiday.[8] The princess was also given a reward of 30,000 gold pieces by her delighted father.[9] Aurangzeb paid
2 CONC  the princely sum of 30,000 gold pieces to the ustani bi for having taught his cherished daughter well.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Zeb-un-Nissa then learned the sciences of the time with Mohammad Saeed Ashraf Mazandarani, who was also a great Persian poet.[11] She learned philosophy, mathematics, astronomy,[12] literature, and was a mistress of Persian, Arabi
2 CONC c and Urdu. She had a good reputation in calligraphy as well.[13] Her library surpassed all other private collections, and she employed many scholars on liberal salaries to produce literary works at her bidding or to copy manuscri
2 CONC pts for her.[9] Her library also provided literary works on each subject, such as law, literature, history and theology.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Zeb-un-Nissa was a kind-hearted person and always helped people in need. She helped widows and orphans. Not only did she help people but every year she sent Hajj pilgrims to Mecca and Medina.[15] She also took an interest in music
2 CONC  and it was said that she was the best singer among the women of her time.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aurangzeb's accession
2 CONT
2 CONT When Aurangzeb became the emperor after Shah Jahan, Zeb-un-Nissa was 21 years old. Aurangzeb found out about the talent and capacity of his daughter and began to discuss the political affairs of his Empire with her, listening to h
2 CONC er opinions. It has been mentioned in some books that Aurangzeb sent all the royal princes for the reception of Zeb-un-Nisa each time she entered the court. Zeb-un-Nissa had four other younger sisters: Zeenat-un-Nissa, Zubdat-un-N
2 CONC issa, Badr-un-Nissa and Mehr-un-Nissa.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...............
