1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yegor (Gregory) Alexandrovich Gruzinsky (1762–1852) was a prince of the Bagration family. He was born on November 2, 1762, in Moscow, into the family of the Georgian tsarevich Alexander Bakarovich Gruzinsky (1726–1
2 CONC 791) and his wife Darya Alexandrovna Menshikova (1747–1817). He was the grandson of King Bakar III Vakhtangovich (1699–1750), who had been in Russian service from 1724 and had earned the rank of lieutenant general of artillery.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received an excellent home education, knew Georgian, French, German, and Italian, and studied history, geography, mathematics, physics, fortification, artillery, and architecture. At a young age, by order of Empress Catherine I
2 CONC I, he was brought to Saint Petersburg, where he was raised under the supervision of his aunt, Elizaveta Bakarovna Gruzinskaya. His father, who had taken part in court intrigues on the side of Peter III, had been forbidden to live 
2 CONC in the capital or to come closer than 100 versts to Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1768, he was enrolled for military service in the Saint Petersburg Infantry Regiment, but he did not enter active service, and in 1788 he retired with the rank of major. He owned estates in Moscow, in the Balakhna, Makaryev, an
2 CONC d Semyonov districts, and in Nizhny Novgorod. He became widely known for his unrestrained temperament, as well as for the “royal” order he established on his estate of Lyskovo in Nizhny Novgorod Province.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruzinsky’s patrimonial estate was located on the right bank of the Volga, opposite the Makaryev Monastery and the famous Nizhny Novgorod Fair. On the opening day of the fair, the prince would arrive at the monastery in a heavily 
2 CONC gilded carriage, drawn by twelve horses in line and accompanied by postilions. Until his arrival, no one dared begin the church service, and all the clergy, headed by the archimandrite, together with all the officials, headed by t
2 CONC he governor, waited for the arrival of the “Tsar of Lyskovo.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The prince’s usual daily occupation consisted of judging and punishing people both subject and not subject to his authority. He was often chosen as a judge or mediator in disputes and lawsuits among landowners, merchants, and peas
2 CONC ants. The latter were most often satisfied with his decisions, loved him in their own way, and called him “Prince Yegor.” It was said that runaway peasants who came under Gruzinsky’s protection were given flour and groats free of 
2 CONC charge, and that craftsmen were always paid properly for their work.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of his lack of restraint, Prince Gruzinsky made many envious enemies. His ill-wishers deliberately distorted facts, trying to discredit him before the provincial and capital authorities. He was even accused of organizing b
2 CONC ands of robbers who plundered merchant vessels on the Volga. When the provincial authorities’ patience finally ran out and the case concerning his arbitrary conduct reached Emperor Paul I, the prince, for an enormous bribe, staged
2 CONC  a magnificent funeral for himself and was considered dead for a full three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of Alexander I’s reign, Gruzinsky received an amnesty and was granted the title of chamberlain. Thanks to his fiery temperament and brilliant organizational abilities, he was elected Marshal of the Nizhny Novgorod
2 CONC  Nobility seven times. In 1812, he initiated the formation of a people’s militia, was elected its commander, and maintained it at his own expense.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fair was transferred to Nizhny Novgorod, the prince settled in the center of the city, in his enormous mansion with a park extending around it for more than a verst. He continued to live as he pleased, disregarding laws 
2 CONC and ranks, for which he was removed from the office of Marshal of the Nobility by imperial decree of Nicholas I.
2 CONT
2 CONT He spent the remainder of his life at his estate in Lyskovo, where he died in 1852 at the age of 89. It was said that he died of a nervous shock when he saw a peasant who had failed to remove his cap before him. He was buried in t
2 CONC he tomb of the Georgian princes in the Transfiguration Cathedral. He was awarded the Order of Saint Vladimir, 4th class.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the book Remarkable Eccentrics and Originals by Mikhail Ivanovich Pylyaev (1842–1899), we read:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Prince Yegor Alexandrovich Gruzinsky, who lived at the beginning of the present century on his affluent Volga estate in the village of Lyskovo, Nizhny Novgorod Province, considered himself a kind of philanthropist. He imagined th
2 CONC at he had been sent to Earth to protect all the poor and oppressed, and therefore received all fugitives and unfortunate people with great hospitality. He also sheltered serfs who had fled from his neighbors because they were diss
2 CONC atisfied in some way. The number of such people under the protection of this affluent prince grew to several hundred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lyskovo was known for hundreds of versts around as a hospitable refuge, though admission was granted with great selectiveness and only after it had been verified that the person seeking help was not a thief or murderer, but merely
2 CONC  an ordinary wanderer. The prince inspired almost terror in the district police: beneath his house he had underground chambers, and in the forests there were mysterious encampments. The prince was harsh in character, and his judgm
2 CONC ent over fugitives was at times more than cruel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Dolgoruky says of him that this man was a daring brawler: he interfered in everyone’s affairs, judged and ordered things arbitrarily, and proved everyone’s guilt and his own rightness with fundamental Russian arguments — th
2 CONC at is, with his fists. He might knock out one person’s eye, tear out another’s beard: such was His Serene Highness’s justice. None of the inhabitants of the province dared complain about him; everyone was intimidated by the splend
2 CONC or of his name. His village was full of fugitives; under his protection they traded, settled there, and no one dared disturb them. The local government knew all this, but remained silent, because otherwise, through his connections
2 CONC , the prince would stir up such storm clouds that no one would escape them.”
