1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismail_III
2 CONT
2 CONT Ismail III (possibly 1733 - 1773), the Safavid shadow king, was the last king of Safavid dynasty. He had no real power. His father was Mirza Morteza, who was a former court official and his mother was a daughter of Shah Sultan Hus
2 CONC ayn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Torab (Persian: ابوتراب‎), better known by his dynastic name of Ismail III (اسماعیل), was a Safavid prince, who reigned as a figurehead under the authority of Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari briefly from 1750 to 1751, and then under
2 CONC  the Zand ruler Karim Khan from 1751 till his death in 1773.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Torab's father was Mirza Morteza, who was a former court official, and his mother was a daughter of the former Safavid king (shah) Sultan Husayn (r. 1694-1722).
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 1750, the Chahar Lang chieftain Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari and the Zand chieftain Karim Khan stormed the gates of Isfahan—its governor Abu'l-Fath Khan Bakhtiari and other prominent residents assembled to protect the fortress
2 CONC  of the city, but agreed to surrender and collaborate with them after Ali Mardan's reasonable proposals.[1] Abu'l-Fath, together with Ali Mardan and Karim Khan, formed an alliance in western Iran under the cover of restoring the S
2 CONC afavid dynasty, appointing the 17 year old Abu Torab as a puppet ruler—on June 29, Abu Torab was declared shah, and assumed the dynastic name of Ismail III.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ali Mardan then took the title of Vakil-e daulat ("deputy of the state")[2] as the head of the administration, while Abu'l-Fath maintained his post as governor of Isfahan, and Karim Khan was appointed commander (sardar) of the arm
2 CONC y, and was given the task of conquering the rest of Iran. Ali Mardan eventually broke the alliance by killing Abu'l-Fath and extracting heavy tax on the inhabitants.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1751, Karim Khan returned to Isfahan and restored order in the city. A battle shortly occurred between him and Ali Mardan in Luristan—during the battle, Ismail III and Zakariya Khan (who was now his vizier), together wi
2 CONC th several prominent officers, deserted Ali Mardan and joined Karim Khan, who eventually emerged victorious, forcing Ali Mardan and the remains of his men, together with the governor of Luristan, Ismail Khan Feyli, to retreat to K
2 CONC huzestan.[3] After Karim Khan's accession as vakil (regent) in the same year, Ismail was kept in safe custody at the stronghold of Abadeh, where he lived till his death, in 1773.[4][5]
