1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Raza Ali Khan''' (1809 – 1866) was an Indian Islamic scholar, Sufi, and anti-colonial figure from Bareilly, in the North-Western Provinces of British India. He is remembered for his contributions to traditional 
2 CONC Islamic scholarship and for his participation in the armed resistance against British colonial forces. He was the father of Maulana Naqi Ali Khan and the grandfather of Imam Ahmad Raza Khan Barelvi. His shift from administrative s
2 CONC ervice to full-time religious scholarship marked a defining transition in the Barelvi family tradition.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Raza Ali Khan''' was born in 1809 (1224 AH) in Bareilly. He belonged to a well-known family of scholars and nobles whose ancestors had migrated from Kandahar during the Mughal period. His father was Maulana Kaazim Ali Khan, a t
2 CONC ax officer in Badayun, and his grandfather was Maulana Muhammad Azam Khan. The family had previously held civil and military roles in the service of the Mughal Empire but gradually shifted toward religious and scholarly pursuits. 
2 CONC Raza Ali Khan was educated in traditional Islamic sciences and studied under Molwi Khaleerur Rahman, receiving qualifications in fiqh, hadith, tafsir, and logic by the age of 23.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his lifetime, Raza Ali Khan witnessed the declining authority of the Mughal Empire and the rising power of the British East India Company. In the early 1830s, he took part in armed resistance against British colonial forces
2 CONC , fighting alongside General Bakht Khan during the 1834 uprisings. However, his later years were devoted to religious scholarship and Sufism. He did not hold any government position, instead focusing on spiritual teaching and inte
2 CONC llectual work in Bareilly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raza Ali Khan was regarded as a person of piety, humility, and deep knowledge. He was a practising Sufi and passed on spiritual teachings within his household. He played a central role in the early education of his son Naqi Ali Kh
2 CONC an and helped lay the intellectual foundation that would shape later generations of scholars in the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1866 (1282 AH) in the Islamic month of Jumada al-Awwal, at the age of 57. His grandson, Ahmad Raza Khan, was ten years old at the time. Raza Ali Khan’s legacy is preserved through his descendants, particularly through t
2 CONC he scholarly and spiritual contributions of his son and grandsons.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Raza Ali Khan and his FIRST wife;'''
2 CONT * 1. Khanum, his eldest daughter.
2 CONT * 2. Naqi Ali Khan, his eldest son, a prominent scholar and author.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Raza Ali Khan and his SECOND wife;'''
2 CONT * 1. Mustajab Begum
2 CONT * 2. Bibi Jaan
2 CONT
