1 TITL Maulana
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Maulana Naqi Ali Khan''' (c. 1830 – 1880) was an Indian Sunni Hanafi scholar and author from Bareilly, in the North-Western Provinces of British India. He is best known as the father of Imam Ahmad Raza Khan Bare
2 CONC lvi and for his own scholarly contributions to Islamic theology, jurisprudence, and devotional literature. Naqi Ali Khan wrote over fifty works and was an influential figure in defending traditional Sunni beliefs during a period o
2 CONC f growing sectarian tensions in colonial India.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Naqi Ali Khan''' was born into a distinguished family of scholars in Bareilly around 1830. His father, Maulana Raza Ali Khan, was a prominent Sufi and scholar who had fought alongside General Bakht Khan against the British in t
2 CONC he early 19th century. Naqi Ali Khan received his early education directly under his father and followed the Dars-i-Nizami curriculum, which included Islamic law, Arabic grammar, logic, theology, and prophetic traditions. The fami
2 CONC ly traced its lineage back to Maulana Sa'eedullah Khan, a former Mughal official who had migrated from Kandahar and settled in Lahore before his descendants moved to Bareilly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naqi Ali Khan began his scholarly career in Bareilly, where he taught students and wrote extensively. His works focused on theology (aqidah), law (fiqh), and defence of Sunni orthodoxy. He was particularly known for writing refuta
2 CONC tions of Wahhabi, Deobandi, and Qadiani doctrines, which he viewed as heretical departures from mainstream Sunni Islam. One of his most famous books is Suroor al-Quloob fi Zikr Mawlood al-Mahboob, which supports the celebration of
2 CONC  the Prophet Muhammad’s birth (Mawlid), a practice widely observed among Indian Sunnis but criticised by reformist movements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though not politically active, Naqi Ali Khan lived during a time of significant upheaval, including the aftermath of the 1857 Indian Rebellion. Unlike some of his ancestors who had held administrative or military roles, he focused
2 CONC  solely on religious life. His home in Bareilly became a centre of Islamic learning and spiritual instruction. He married a girl named Hussaini Khanum. He personally supervised the early education of his first son, Ahmad Raza Khan
2 CONC , and is said to have recognised his son's extraordinary intelligence and spiritual inclination from a young age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naqi Ali Khan died in 1880 at the age of approximately fifty. His passing occurred when his son Ahmad Raza Khan was twenty-four years old. Despite his relatively early death, his intellectual and spiritual legacy continued through
2 CONC  his son, who became one of the most influential Sunni scholars in South Asia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Naqi Ali Khan and his wife Hussaini Khanum:''' Two known sons
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. Ahmed Raza Khan
2 CONT * 2. Hassan Raza Khan
2 CONT
