1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ahmad Raza Khan Barelvi''' (born 14 June 1856, died 28 October 1921) was a prominent Islamic scholar, jurist, theologian, poet, and spiritual leader from British India. Known respectfully as A’la Hazrat ("The Gr
2 CONC and Master"), he was the founder of the Barelvi movement, which has had a profound influence on Sunni Islam in South Asia and beyond.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ahmad Raza Khan''' was born in the city of Bareilly in the North-Western Provinces of British India (modern-day Uttar Pradesh, India). His family traced its ancestry back to the Pashtun tribe of Barech, originally from Kandahar
2 CONC  in Afghanistan. His forefather, Muhammad Saeedullah Khan, migrated from Kandahar during the early 18th century after serving as a military commander under the Persian ruler Nader Shah. This migration marked the beginning of the f
2 CONC amily's long residence in South Asia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmad Raza’s father, '''Naqi Ali Khan''' (1830–1880), was a well-respected Islamic scholar and spiritual guide, who played a crucial role in Ahmad Raza’s early education and religious upbringing. His mother, Muhammad Harif Khanam,
2 CONC  came from a family similarly rooted in Islamic scholarship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmad Raza was born on '''14 June 1856''' (10 Shawwal 1272 AH). His birth name was Muhammad, but he was also known by the title Abd-ul-Mustafa, meaning "Servant of the Chosen One." The family home was located in the old quarter of
2 CONC  Bareilly, where he received his initial education.
2 CONT
2 CONT From a young age, Ahmad Raza Khan showed exceptional intelligence and dedication to religious studies. His father personally guided much of his early training in Quranic studies, Arabic grammar, Islamic jurisprudence (fiqh), and t
2 CONC heology (aqeedah). By the time he was 22 years old, Ahmad Raza became a disciple of Syed Shah Aale Rasul Marehrawi (d. 1879), a prominent Sufi saint and scholar. Under his spiritual guidance, Ahmad Raza received authorisation (khi
2 CONC lafat) to teach and guide others, becoming affiliated with several Sufi orders including the Qadiri, Chishti, and Naqshbandi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other notable teachers who shaped his scholarship include:
2 CONT * ''Syed Shah Aale Rasul Marehrawi'' (d. 1879)
2 CONT * ''Ahmad Zayni Dahlan'' of Mecca (d. 1881), a leading authority in Hadith studies.
2 CONT * ''Abdul Rahman Siraj Makki'' (d. 1883)
2 CONT * ''Abul Hussain Ahmad al-Nuri'' (d. 1906)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT His formal education covered a wide range of Islamic sciences, including Quranic exegesis (tafsir), Hadith, logic, philosophy, and jurisprudence, specialising in the Hanafi school.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ahmad Raza Khan was a prolific writer, composing over 1,000 works in Arabic, Persian, and Urdu. Of these, around 679 have been preserved and catalogued.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''His most famous and influential works include:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Fatawa Razawiyya (1892–1921): A massive collection of Islamic legal verdicts spread over approximately 30 volumes and 22,000 pages. It addressed everyday religious questions, detailed jurisprudential debates, and practical rulin
2 CONC gs on issues such as marriage, inheritance, trade, and worship.
2 CONT * Kanz-ul-Iman: An Urdu translation and detailed commentary on the Quran, presenting the text with Hanafi jurisprudential views. This work has been translated into multiple languages such as English, Bengali, Turkish, Dutch, Sindh
2 CONC i, and Gujarati.
2 CONT * Husam al-Haramain: A treatise written around 1909 which condemned theological positions of rival groups such as the Deobandi and Ahl-i Hadith movements. It was signed by 268 scholars across South Asia, Mecca, and Medina.
2 CONT * Hadaiq-e-Bakhshish: A famous collection of na’at poetry (poetry in praise of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ), celebrated for its spiritual depth and literary merit.
2 CONT * Al Dawlatul Makkiya bil Madatul Ghaibiya (1905): A work written while on pilgrimage in Mecca, refuting Wahhabi claims regarding divine knowledge of the unseen, composed despite suffering from illness. Ahmad Raza also engaged wit
2 CONC h scientific ideas of his time, authoring Fauze Mubeen Dar Radde Harkate Zameen in 1902, which argued against the theory of Earth’s rotation based on his reading of scripture and science.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ahmad Raza Khan’s spiritual mission was to uphold the Sunni orthodox tradition as understood through the Hanafi school, incorporating Sufi spirituality. His movement, now known as the Barelvi movement, stresses the deep reverence 
2 CONC for Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, including belief in his continuing spiritual presence (Haazir wa Naazir), his unique spiritual light (nur), and intercessory power. He actively opposed movements such as Wahhabism, Deobandism, Ahmadiyya, an
2 CONC d Shi’ism, which he believed distorted orthodox Sunni teachings. He declared Wahhabis as outside the fold of Islam due to their rejection of many traditional practices, labelled Ahmadiyyas as heretics, and challenged Deobandi and 
2 CONC Shia theological points in detailed polemics.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his lifetime, Ahmad Raza Khan was wary of political movements that might destabilise Muslim society or force drastic change. He opposed the Indian National Congress-led independence struggle because he did not consider it i
2 CONC n the Muslim community's best interests. He maintained that British India was Dar al-Islam, where Muslims could practise their faith freely. His position on political issues was cautious and religiously conservative, focusing on p
2 CONC reserving Islamic identity and spirituality rather than nationalist politics. However, after his death, many Barelvi followers became active in the Pakistan Movement seeking a Muslim homeland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ahmad Raza Khan''' passed away on 28 October 1921 (16 Safar 1340 AH) in Bareilly. His shrine there is a major centre of pilgrimage, especially during his Urs (death anniversary), attracting thousands of devotees from across the
2 CONC  subcontinent and abroad. His legacy continues through numerous seminaries (madrassas), religious institutions, and organisations like Jamat Raza-e-Mustafa, which promote his teachings and works. His influence is evident across So
2 CONC uth Asia and diaspora communities worldwide.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Ahmed Raza Khan Barelvi and his wife Irshad Begum: Five sons, and two daughters'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hamid Raza Khan, Islamic scholar and mystic
2 CONT * Mustafa Raza Khan, Islamic scholar and author
2 CONT * Mustafai Begum
2 CONT * Kaneez Hasan
2 CONT * Kaneez Husain
2 CONT * Kaneez Hasnain
2 CONT * Murtazai Begum
2 CONT
