0 @N6000000001247177021@ NOTE History of Washington, Louisiana
1 CONT
1 CONT Nestled in Northern St. Landry Parish, Louisiana, Washington is one of the oldest settlements in Louisiana.  Originally known as "Church Landing", the Town of Washington dates back as early as 1774.  With the distinction as Louisi
1 CONC ana's third oldest settlement, many of Washington's homes and building are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.  Antebellum homes, bed and breakfasts, and over 100 antique markets make Washington a unique cultural e
1 CONC xperience.   
1 CONT
1 CONT Just eight miles north of Opelousas and 29 miles from Lafayette, Washington is easily accessible from Interstate 49, exit 25.  For a scenic "Cajun Country" experience, drive through Washington on the Zydeco Cajun Prairie Scenic By
1 CONC way. 
1 CONT
1 CONT First established as a French trading post, the area known as Poste des Opelousas was originally granted to Jacques Courtableau.  This community was later called Church Landing and included the first church in the area, La Iglesia
1 CONC  Paroquial de la Immaculada Conception del Puesto de Opelousas, which was built in 1774.  The Town of Washington, as it is known today, was incorporated in 1835.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the banks of Bayou Courtableau, the community became an important steamboat port, shipping cotton, cattle , and sugar.  It became the largest steamboat port between New Orleans and St. Louis, Missourie.  With the arrival of the
1 CONC  railroad in 1883, the once important steamboat port began to decline.  The last steamboat departed in 1900.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Although other areas of the state have surpassed the little community on Bayou Courtableau, Washington has never lost the character of its rich heritage.
1 CONT
1 CONT ****************************************************************************************************
1 CONT WASHINGTON
1 CONT
1 CONT The town of Washington on Bayou Courtableau is the jewel in the 
1 CONT crown of St. Landry Parish. It was originally called Church's 
1 CONT Landing because it was the site of the first Catholic Church in the 
1 CONT parish, built in 1770. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Once the second largest port in Louisiana and a major inland 
1 CONT commercial route for the area, Washington was a thriving steamboat 
1 CONT town in its heyday. Steamboat service began in the 1820s after Bayou 
1 CONT Courtableau and the Atchafalaya River were cleared of obstruction. 
1 CONT Horse carts and boats from area plantations brought in agricultural 
1 CONT products including sugar, cotton, and livestock. At Washington, 
1 CONT everything was loaded onto steamers to be shipped to New Orleans 
1 CONT through a myriad of bayous, rivers, and inlets leading to the 
1 CONT Atchafalaya River. 
1 CONT
1 CONT With the heavy commercial activity during the steamboat days came 
1 CONT the necessary structures: warehouses, shops, and homes for the 
1 CONT planters and steamboat captains. Much of the original town is 
1 CONT included in the Washington Historic District, which is on the 
1 CONT National Register of Historic Places. 
1 CONT
1 CONT According to National Register documents, the town contains a large 
1 CONT number of fine examples of Louisiana house types, as well as a 
1 CONT number of late 19th-century commercial buildings including a 
1 CONT steamboat warehouse, a brick warehouse, and several shops that 
1 CONT retain their original ornamental shop fronts. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Take a minute to visit the Washington Museum and Tourist Information 
1 CONT Center to find out about everything Washington has to offer. Then 
1 CONT stroll down the narrow, tree-lines streets of this beautiful town 
1 CONT for a sublime slip into the past. The lovely homes, many of them 
1 CONT carefully restored, add to the 19th-century feel. Antiques reign 
1 CONT supreme here, so plan to spend some time in the shops on Main Street 
1 CONT and in the antique mall in the old Washington High School. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Virginia may have its Williamsburg, but Louisiana can proudly claim 
1 CONT Washington as her own piece of early America. 
1 CONT
1 CONT WASHINGTON MUSEUM & TOURIST INFORMATION
1 CONT 404 North Main St.; (337) 826-3627; www.townofwashingtonla.org; Mon-
1 CONT Fri 8 am-4 pm, Sat-Sun 9 am-4pm. Make this your first stop in 
1 CONT Washington for information on historic sites, maps of the area, 
1 CONT house tours, walking tours, antique shops, and bed-and-breakfast 
1 CONT listings. More than 80 percent of this once thriving steamboat town 
1 CONT is listed on the National Register of Historic Places. Many 
1 CONT interesting artifacts of the steamboat era are housed in the museum. 
1 CONT
1 CONT *********************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT **********************
1 CONT
1 CONT Note in records:
1 CONT
1 CONT GRADENIGO, Jean of Venice (Antoine & Marie Bettio) m. 17 March 1766 Marguerite Kreps (Hugues Rene Krebs & Marie Josephe LaPointe) (See:  Vidrine, Jacqueline O. in "Love's Legacy:  The Mobile Marriages... 1724-1786"  published in 1
1 CONC 985; p.345)
1 CONT
1 CONT ***************************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT From: Kathryn E Herzog <BUSFRIEND@prodigy.net>
1 CONT To: annette_lockett@msn.com <annette_lockett@msn.com>
1 CONT Subject: Gradinego connection
1 CONT Date: Saturday, July 25, 1998 9:32 AM
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi Annette,
1 CONT
1 CONT A friend of mine showed me your home page.  I checked my geneological record
1 CONT and saw that my great...great grandmother Aimee Agatha Gradinego was the
1 CONT daughter of Jean Gradenigo.  Jean,  who was a native of Venice,  married
1 CONT Marguerite Krebs in 1766 and settled in St. Landry Parish.  Aimee Agatha
1 CONT Gradenigo married Cornelius Voorhies in 1863.  Aimee was the 7th of 9
1 CONT children.  Other siblings in order are:  Augustin,  Hilaire, Joseph,
1 CONT Marguerite, Louisa, Bridget, Marie,  and Antoine.    Perhaps your Augustine
1 CONT is a child of one of these Gradenigo children.  All of Aimee and Cornelius'
1 CONT children were born in Avoyelles Parish.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Gradenigo palace still stands in Venice.  It reverted to the State,
1 CONT after the death of an uncle of Jean Gradenigo.  My grandmother traced the
1 CONT line back to 697 A.D.  The line,  which is loosely documented and includes
1 CONT an assortment of Dukes,  begins with Pietro Gradenigo.  The Gradenigo family
1 CONT left Aquileya at the time of Attila's raids,  settled at Grado,  which they
1 CONT built and took its name.  It is one of the 24 families who instituted the
1 CONT Venetian Patriciate.  The family has had among its members three Doges,
1 CONT several Procurators of St. Mark's (note: 2nd highest office),  Patriarchs,
1 CONT Bishops,  Prelates,  Generals, Senators, Ambassadors,  and Knights.  (This
1 CONT information is taken from a photostat of the original genealogical tree in a
1 CONT manuscript by Capellarie,  "Il Campidogio Veneto," preserved in the Marciana
1 CONT Library of Venice.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Back to Jean:  Jean Gradenigo was the son of Antonio Gradenigo and Marcia
1 CONT (last name unknown).  The uncle's name was Bishop Giovanni Gradenigo.
1 CONT Giovanni was descended from Doge (of Venice) Pietro Gradenigo,  who drove
1 CONT Bajamonte Tiepolo and the Quirini family from Venice when they attempted to
1 CONT seize the Piazza in 1310.    The will of the late Very Rev. Giovanni
1 CONT Gradenigo,  which included his vast estate amounting to nearly $80 million,
1 CONT from the last Gradenigo Doge,  left one third to the Holy Roman Catholic
1 CONT Church,  one third to a nephew in Florence,  Italy,  and one third to his
1 CONT nephew,  Jean Gradenigo,  above mentioned,  who came to America and resided
1 CONT in Opelousas,  LA.  It was eventually learned that the estate was
1 CONT distributed to the poor of the city of Venice,  the poor of the parish of
1 CONT St. Marco and for the celebration of Masses.
1 CONT
1 CONT I hope this helps.  I'm interested to know how you are connected to this
1 CONT family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kathryn Herzog
1 CONT busfriend@prodigy.net
1 CONT
1 CONT ********************************************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT 1/26/2001
1 CONT
1 CONT This information was found on the internet.  This is information found by a researcher in a typed document included with the Krebs family information.  It reads thus:
1 CONT
1 CONT [To Krebs Family.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Gradenigo moved to Opelousas, where his name appears, in 1796 as president of the trustees of the church of the Immaculate Conception.  In a marginal note in the church records of Opelousas the parish priest, Father Barriere,
1 CONC  gives the following account of him.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Juan de Gradenigo was born in Venice, the son of a very distinguished family.  His uncle was a Canon of St. Mark's Cathedral of Venice.  A large number of the male members of this family served the Republic of Venice in the capac
1 CONC ity of senators and ambassadors.  One of the paternal ancestors was even Doge of that world-famed commercial Republic in 1356.  When still a young man, Juan took a liking to traveling.  He visited the whole of Europe and traveled 
1 CONC extensively in North and South America.  During one of these trips he came to Mobile, where he fell in love with a Miss Margarite Krebs, the daughter of a rich German of that place.  A son was born to the couple in December 1781, 
1 CONC and baptized November 17, 1782.  Later on the boy was sent to Venice to his kin people, where he was to acquire an education in keeping with the traditions of this illustrious family, and for which there was no opportunity in the 
1 CONC colonies.  When Napoleon was sent to Egypt by the Directoire, young Juan ran away from his kin, bid adieu to the city of the Lagoons and followed the Corsican adventurer into the land of the Pharaos.  There, in the burning sands a
1 CONC t the foot of the pyramids, he is supposed to have perished.  No news ever reached the anxiously waiting parents in the far-away post of the Opelousas."
1 CONT
1 CONT Barriere winds up this very interesting bit of family history with the words: "Don Martin Duralde and myself were particular friends of Mr. De Gradenigo.  Still, we never tried to solve the mystery which surrounded his emigration 
1 CONC to La.  He died in this country (Opelousas) and was buried March 1, 1809.
1 CONT
1 CONT L. R. Sentis, "The Morning Star" Feb. 29th 1908]
1 CONT
1 CONT *******************************************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT                              Another interesting note passed on to me.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Jean Francois Gradenigo married Marguerite Krebs.  She lived in Pascagoula, in the DeLaPoint home, which her mother had inherited.  (Pascagoula is in what is now Jackson County, Mississippi.)  He arrived in Pensaciola as a coloni
1 CONC st, met Marguerite in Pascagoula, and married her.  During this period of time, the flag flying over Pascagoula changed several times, and the capital of the French providence moved -- from Pascagoula to Mobile to Biloxi to New Or
1 CONC leans.  (In fact, Marguerite's father, Hugo Ernestus Krebs, filed his will in New Orleans, at the St. Louis cathedral, since by that time it was the seat of government for the French colony.)  So, although the marriage and births 
1 CONC are recorded in Mobile and Pensacola, they actually took place in Pascagoula, and were recorded in the churches in the other towns.  .......... The first (and correct) spelling of Marguerite's last name is Krebs. Marguerite had
1 CONT  half-brothers and sisters who ended up in Indian Territory, where "les Americains" began misspelling the name. In records made by English-speakers, the name is variously spelled Creps, Crebs, Kreps, Krepps, and even Crabs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite and Jean made their way to the Opelousas post, in what is now St. Landry Parish,  Louisiana. They and their family members appear in the census, court, and church records there. There is an 1813 French census of the lan
1 CONC ds of Louis Carriere which lists them and their family. They are also mentioned in the 1810 Census of St. Landry Parish. (The GenWeb site for St. Landry Parish, Louisiana, has a lot info available online for this area.)
1 CONT
1 CONT  Jean and Marguerite had a son Augustin, whose birth is noted in the Pensacola  records for 1772; he died in St. Landry Parish (Louisiana) before 1823; he served  in the war of 1812. He married twice, and had a namesake son born 6
1 CONC  Dec. 1811.  Other children of Marguerite and Jean Gradenigo were Marie (born abt 1774),  Margarita (born after 1776, d 1855 or 1856 in St. Landry Parish), Louisa (born  abt 1778), Aimee Agatha (born about 1780), Antonio Donato (b
1 CONC orn Dec 17, 1781), Hilario (born Jan 3, 1784), Brigida (born 8 March, 1787) , and Jorge (born  abt 1801).
1 CONT
1 CONT Some other info to keep in mind.....In 1700 and for some years afterward, French  Louisiana included Mobile, Pascagoula, New Orleans, and points West and  North.... The French were fond of taking frequent censuses of smaller areas
1 CONC ,  sometimes of the "lands of " a particular landowner........Slavery under the French  at this time was a different system than took hold in the US later. Slavery was considered a temporary condition, and there were legal means f
1 CONC or slaves to buy  their freedom, and this was done frequently, as attested to in the records. The French also named "Free People of Color", who were not slaves, and who could own land and transact business, including buying and se
1 CONC lling slaves (You'll see reference to a Kreps Gradenigo, F.P.C., in the St. Landry records -- seems a colorful guy, but I haven't been able to figure out where he fits in the tree)......Marguerite was what is termed a "Creole" in 
1 CONC Louisiana. She was born in  the Colony, of a German/Alsatian colonist father, and a mother of French-Canadian and continental French parentage. Marguerite and her family  were French-speaking.........Jean Gradenigo's name appears 
1 CONC in the records of the Church of the Immaculate Conception, in 1796, as president of the trustees of the  church; the parish priest was Fr. Barriere.
1 CONT
1 CONT **********************************************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT Augustine Grandenigo (Sr., b. abt 1772) married twice: first to Elizabeth  Montchervaux Clermont (she appears in the 1810 Census); and secondly he married Genevieve Jacques Fontenot."
1 CONT
1 CONT *********************************************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT The date of the birth of Juan de Gradenigo was surmised from the age on his burial records from St. Landry Catholic Church.  He has land dealings between 1763 and 1772 in Pensacola, Florida. ( Louisiana State Archives in Baton Rou
1 CONC ge.  Louisiana state Archives.  British Colonial Records)  Jerome Matulich and Juan de Gradenigo were partners in numerous land dealings in he area just north of present day Pensacola, Florida.  He signed a will on September 6, 18
1 CONC 06 in Opelousas, Louisiana.  (Daily World - St. Landry Parish 150th Anniversary Edition page 6) Nobleman Juan di Gradenigo, a man of Venice who settled in St. Landry Parish, made his last will and testament, in his own hand, in Fr
1 CONC ench, on September 6, 1806.  His will was filled on March 20, 1809 following his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Juan di Gradenigo.  Opelousas Judge George King on March 18, 1809, issued an appointment to Aaron Reeder to swear in the appriaisers of the Gradenigo estate.  Gradenigo had come here from Venice and acquired and
1 CONC  settled a large plantation northwest of Opelousas and the section was called "Gradenigo's Island" -- a name applied to a grove of trees rising up in the prairie.  He died on March 1, 1809 in Opelousas, Louisiana.  (All Catholic C
1 CONC hurch Records - Baptism, Confirmation, Marriages and Burials.  St. Landry Catholic church Opelousas, La. Burial Records, Vol. 1 Pg. 101)  He was buried on March 1, 1809 in Opelousas, Louisiana. (St. Landry Catholic Church Burial R
1 CONC ecords vol 1 page 101 Opelousas, Louisiana.) Juan di Gradenigo was buried by Fr. Louis Buchot of St. Landry Catholic Church - Opelousas, Louisiana.  He was Italian from Venice, Italy.  (Phone book of Venice Italy 1990.)  Juan de G
1 CONC radenigo came to Pensacola Florida when the Spanish founded the city.  He was in the service of the Spanish Government until his retirement in 1776.  At that time the de Gradenigo received a Spanish land grant in Opelousas, Louisi
1 CONC ana.
1 CONT
1 CONT There are still in Venice today, members of the de Gradnigio Family.
1 CONT
1 CONT ************************************************************************************************
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo a S. Giustina
1 CONT
1 CONT Date: 17th century 
1 CONT Address: Castello 2838 
1 CONT Current use: Venis SpA 
1 CONT Overview map: locate 
1 CONT
1 CONT Description:
1 CONT This 16th century palace was lately restored with great efforts and now serves as legal seat of the company 'Venis SpA'. The building with a L-type plan (courtyard behind the left wing) still has several ceiling and wall frescoes 
1 CONC from the late 18th and early 19th century, partially are attributed to G. Scajario. An engraving by Coronelli shows the palace with different windows and a missing last floor. The right water gate had a rectangular form, the four-
1 CONC light loggia did not exist. It is doubtful whether Coronelli's engraving, which shows the Chiesa di Santa Giustina, shows the palace correctly.
1 CONT Pietro Gradenigo, son of Jacopo (1695-1776), author of the Commemoriali Veneti and other important works, once had a rich library of 350 manuscripts, which was inherited after his death by his nephews Girolamo and Pietro, but late
1 CONC r dispersed.
1 CONT
1 CONT ********************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo a San Simeon Grande
1 CONT
1 CONT Date: 18th century 
1 CONT Architect: Domenico Margutti ? 
1 CONT Address: Santa Croce 764 
1 CONT Current use: private 
1 CONT Overview map: locate 
1 CONT
1 CONT Description:
1 CONT According to Tassini, the building was designed by a scholar of Massari, but Bassi's attribution to Domenico Margutti, a follower of Baldassare Longhena, seems to be more plausible. Frank (2004) even believes in a intervention by 
1 CONC Longhena himself. The asymmetric broad canal façade with a four-light loggia in the piano nobile does only present a right wing. A large gate on the Fondamenta Gradenigo leads to a small courtyard, which borders directly to the an
1 CONC drone. The androne plan is not typical, but more square-like. A enormous fanò with a height of about 2m can be found there. A staircase with marble-incrustated floor leads to the upper floors. Two additional small courtyards provi
1 CONC de light support. Beside the land gate, there is a large garden. In the first piano nobile, two apartments still have late 18th century ceiling and wall frescoes in their stucco, but the second floor suffered horizontal subdivisio
1 CONC ns, except the portego.
1 CONT Spring 2002: restoration works finished. Facade with new white plaster.
1 CONT
1 CONT *************************************************************************************
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo, l'innovazione nella tradizione
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo, situato a S. Giustina nel cuore del sestiere di Castello, ha una storia architettonica ed artistica piuttosto articolata e complessa.
1 CONT L'origine del ramo di S. Giustina dei Gradenigo sarebbe da ricollegarsi a Pietro di Taddeo, figura di qualche rilievo della vita politica veneziana.
1 CONT Tra il 1532 e il 1544 dovrebbe porsi il probabile acquisto, da parte di Giovambattista di Taddeo Gradenigo, fratello di Pietro, dell'edificio, già peraltro rilevato da testimonianze iconografiche.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nel ventennio che corre tra il 1555 ed il 1576 ebbe luogo un primo importante rifacimento.
1 CONT Il pianterreno era costituito da un androne d'ingresso, provvisto a nord di doppio accesso, da terra, tramite il ponticello privato sul campo, e dall'acqua.
1 CONT Nella seconda metà del Settecento Piero e Girolamo Gradenigo commissionarono un ulteriore intervento di ristrutturazione, che si rese necessario in considerazione delle condizioni in cui all'epoca il palazzo versava. 
1 CONT Ampi rifacimenti coinvolsero i due piani superiori, distanziando sul prospetto verso campo S. Giustina i finestroni originariamente binati. Fu realizzata la sopraelevazione di un piano che dovette rendere indispensabile un notevol
1 CONC e irrobustimento delle fondazioni.
1 CONT
1 CONT Una serie di aggiornamenti decorative interni, tuttora superstite, che riguarda gli affreschi dei soffitti, le decorazioni a stucco, gli abbassamenti e riquadri a marmorino colorato, può essere datata nei primi anni dell’800.
1 CONT A cavallo della metà del XIX secolo, si può far risalire il ricorso a rivestimenti murari di tessuti o carta da parati su supporti lignei.
1 CONT
1 CONT Il recente accurato restauro ha ridato l’originale dignità al Palazzo Gradenigo, attraverso un’operazione complessa di conservazione integrale e di reintegro di tutti gli elementi quali i decori, gli affreschi dei soffitti, i pavi
1 CONC menti in calce alla veneziana e le vetrate a piombo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Il Palazzo, per la destinazione d’uso assegnata, è stato completamente cablato e dotato di una impiantistica altamente sofisticata, che ha permesso di mantenere e preservare il disegno planimetrico originale.
1 CONT
1 CONT (Rough Translation into English)
1 CONT
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo, the innovation in the tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT Palazzo Gradenigo, situated to S. Giustina in the heart of sestiere of Castle, has one articulate and rather complex architectonic and artistic history.
1 CONT The origin of the branch of S. Giustina of the Gradenigo would be from referring itself to Peter di Taddeo, figure of some relief of the veneziana political life.
1 CONT Between 1532 and 1544 would have to porsi the probable purchase, from part of Giovambattista di Taddeo Gradenigo, brother of Peter, of the building, already moreover found from iconografiche testimonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT In ventennio that it runs between 1555 and 1576 it had place a first important rifacimento.
1 CONT The ground floor was constituted from a androne of income, supplied to north of approached double quantity, from earth, through the ponticello private on the field, and from the water.
1 CONT In the second half of the 1700's Piero and Girolamo Gradenigo they commissioned an ulterior participation of restructure, than yields necessary in consideration of the conditions in which to the age palace it poured. 
1 CONT Wide rifacimenti were involved the two advanced plans, distancing on the prospect towards field S. Giustina the finestroni originally twin. The sopraelevazione of a plan was realized that had to render a remarkable strengthening o
1 CONC f the foundations indispensable.
1 CONT
1 CONT A decorative series of modernizations insides, still survivor, that it regards frescoes to it of the ceilings, the decorations to stucco, the colored lowerings and riquadri to marmorino, can be dated in the first years of the 800.
1 CONT To horse of the half of XIX the century, it can be made to go back the resource to woven murari coverings of or paper from adorns to you on lignei supports.
1 CONT
1 CONT The recent one taken care of restoration has ridato originates it them dignity to Palazzo Gradenigo, through a complex operation of integral conservation and of reintegro of all the elements which the honor, frescoes it of the cei
1 CONC lings, the pavements at the bottom to the veneziana and the vetrate ones to lead.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Palace, for the assigned destination of use, has been completely wired and equipped of system a highly sophisticated one, that it has allowed to maintain and to preservare the planimetric design originates them.
1 CONT
1 CONT ****************************************************************************************
1 CONT
1 CONT Gradenigo (Fondamenta) a S. Simeon Grande. Il palazzo Gradenigo è della scuola del Massari. Adiacente al medesimo stendesi ampio giardino, intorno al quale si può girare in carrozza a quattro cavalli, come praticavasi fino allo sp
1 CONC irare del secolo trascorso. In esso si diede una caccia di tori il 10 febbraio 1767 M. V. 
1 CONT La patrizia famiglia Gradenigo è originaria dalla Transilvania, ove teneva posto qualificato, e signoreggiava ampie possessioni, ma, esclusa per ire di parte, trasferissi in Aquileja, e vi dimorò fino alla distruzione di quella ci
1 CONC ttà. Allora, come vogliono alcuni, andò ad edificare Grado, da cui in seguito passò a Venezia. Vanta un Giovanni, che seguì nella fuga il doge Pietro Orseolo, suo suocero, e che, vestito l'abito di S. Benedetto, morì nel 1016 a Mo
1 CONC ntecassino in odore di santità. Vanta pure, in mezzo a molti patriarchi, vescovi, generali, senatori ecc., tre dogi, fra i quali Pietro fece adottare nel 1297 la famosa legge che riformò l'antica costituzione veneta, sostituendo a
1 CONC ll'originaria democrazia l'aristocrazia ereditaria. Questo doge domò eziandio la ribellione di Baiamonte Tiepolo, e morì nel 1311 avendo sepoltura in chiesa di S. Cipriano di Murano, fondata nel 1108 dalla di lui famiglia, e juspa
1 CONC tronato della medesima. 
1 CONT Un'altra linea dei Gradenigo diede il nome ad un «Ramo» e ad una «Corte» situati a S. Giustina. Alla linea da S. Giustina si riferisce il seguente racconto, che trascriviamo dal codice 183, Classe VII della Marciana: «16... Girola
1 CONC mo Gradenigo q. Pietro q. Tadio da S. Giustina era fatto dalla famiglia Ab. di S. Cipriano. Gli fu consegnata e raccomandata dal duca di Mantova la cantatrice D... che cantava nel teatro di S. Moisè, poi a S. Luca, e innamoratosi 
1 CONC fornicò seco, et s'accordò seco, il che venuto all'orecchio del Duca, lo perseguitò talmente che, abbenchè fuggito a Trento per andar in Germania con la donna, gli convenne tornar a dietro, e, per quanto hanno operato li fratelli,
1 CONC  non è mai stato possibile placar il duca, che ha voluto che vada ai suoi piedi a chiedergli perdono, e sposarla in sua presenza, che indegnamente lo eseguì, et anzi con fasto e vanità la conduce in tutti li ricreamenti, abbenchè 
1 CONC per questo sia fuori della sua casa, e membro reciso colla sua parte fuori di casa». 
1 CONT Il palazzo Gradenigo a S. Giustina, la cui fabbrica attuale data dal secolo XVII, era celebre per ricca biblioteca di stampati e di manoscritti, alcuni dei quali compilati nel secolo trascorso da Pietro Gradenigo, nonché pel museo
1 CONC  di monete e di medaglie, formato specialmente a cura di Giacomo Gradenigo q. Girolamo, che fu Provveditore generale in Dalmazia, morto nel 1795, e del di lui fratello Gian Agostino, vescovo di Ceneda. Qui soggiornò ai nostri temp
1 CONC i l'arciduca d'Austria Federico. 
1 CONT Il Cicogna nelle sue schede manoscritte riporta il seguente avanzo d'iscrizione che lesse sui muri del palazzo medesimo: Danieli Gra... Et Cap. 
1 CONT Gradenigo (Rami) detti Chioverette a San Simeon Grande. Sono dietro il palazzo Gradenigo, rammentato nel principio dell'articolo precedente. Sappiamo poi dalla Descrizione della contrada pel 1713 che «Nardo Biasin» teneva a pigion
1 CONC e in questo sito le «chiovere con tre casette e due casotti del N. U. Bortolo Gradenigo». Pel nome «Chioverette» vedi Chioverette (Calle delle). 
1 CONT (ROUGH TRANSLATION TO ENGLISH)
1 CONT Gradenigo (Foundations) to S. Simeon Grande. The Gradenigo palace is of the school of the Massari. Adjacent to same the stendesi wide garden, around to which it can be turned in carrozza four horses, as it was practiced until dyin
1 CONC g of the passed century. In it hunting of 10 Tauruses was given one February 1767 M. V. 
1 CONT Patrizia the Gradenigo family is original from the Transilvania, where it held characterized place, and signoreggiava wide properties, but, excluded for ire of part, you transferred in Aquileja, and it dwelled you until the destru
1 CONC ction of that city. Then, as they want some, it went to build up Degree, from which later on it passed to Venice. It boasts a Giovanni, that she followed in the escape doge Peter Orseolo, its suocero, and that, dressed the dress o
1 CONC f S. Benedict, died in 1016 to Montecassino in odore of holiness. It also boasts, in means to many patriarchs, bishops, generates them, senators etc, three dogi, between which Peter made to adopt in the 1297 famous law that reform
1 CONC ed the ancient constitution veneta, replacing to the original democracy the hereditary aristocracy. This doge tamed eziandio the rebellion of Baiamonte Tiepolo, and died in 1311 having interment in church of S. Cipriano di Murano,
1 CONC  founded in the 1108 from of he the family, and juspatronato of the same one. 
1 CONT An other line of the Gradenigo gave the name to one “Branch” and to one “Short” it situates to you to S. Giustina. To the line from S. Giustina refers the following story, that we transcribe from code 183, Class WAYS of the Marcia
1 CONC na: «16... Girolamo Gradenigo q. Peter q. Tadio from S. Giustina was made from the Ab. family of S. Cipriano. Registered letter from duca of Mantova the cantatrice D was delivered and… that it sang in the theatre of S. Moisè, then
1 CONC  to S. Luca, and innamoratosi fornicò seco, ET s'accordò seco, that come to the ear of the Duca, the perseguitò so that, abbenchè escaped to Trento in order to go in Germany with the woman, convene to it to return to behind, and, 
1 CONC for how much they have never operated siblings, have not been possible placar the duca, that has intentional that go to its feet to chiedergli forgiveness, and to marry it in its presence, than dishonourably it executed it, ET ind
1 CONC eed with pomp and vanity it leads it in all them ricreamenti, abbenchè for this it is outside of its house, and member reciso glue its part outside of house”. 
1 CONT The Gradenigo palace to S. Giustina, whose factory puts into effect given them from century XVII, was celebre for rich library of prints to you and of manuscripts, some of which compile to you in the century passed from Peter Grad
1 CONC enigo, let alone pel museum of coins and medals, format especially edited by Giacomo Gradenigo q. Girolamo, that it was general Provveditore in Dalmatia, died in 1795, and of of he the Gian brother Augustin, bishop of Ceneda. Here
1 CONC  soggiornò to our times the archduke of Austria Federico. 
1 CONT The Cicogna in its written by hand cards the following filler remainder of registration that it read on the walls of the same palace: Danieli Gra… ET CAP. 
1 CONT Gradenigo (Coppers) Chioverette sayings to Saint Simeon Grande. They are behind the Gradenigo palace, reminded in the principle of the previous article. We know then from the Description of contrada pel 1713 that “Nardo Biasin” it
1 CONC  held to pigione in this situated one “to chiovere with three small houses and two casotti of N. U. Bortolo Gradenigo”. Pel name “Chioverette” you see Chioverette (Calle of). 
1 CONT
